{
  "set": "freq-shifter-listening",
  "description": "m39-z, the .freqShifter insert - THE LISTENING SET. Its sibling freq-shifter-physics.json is a measurement fixture (one bare sine, so a Goertzel probe can read single bins); this set exists because a sine moving from 440 Hz to 640 Hz proves the physics and tells you NOTHING about whether you want the effect. The material is the same pluck riff the transient-shaper set used, so the two are comparable by ear. ONE AXIS: the total shift in Hz. Everything else is held - same riff, same synth, mix 0.5 on every shifted variant, outputDb 0, spread 0. Mix is 0.5 rather than 1 because that is how the effect is actually used musically: the original note stays, the shifted copy rings against it, and the BEATING between them is the sound. At mix 1 you hear only the shifted copy and the riff's harmony is simply gone - correct, but it reads as a broken synth rather than as an effect. WHAT TO LISTEN FOR: the shifted copy is INHARMONIC. A pitch shifter would move every partial by the same RATIO and you would hear a second pluck a musical interval away. This moves every partial by the same number of HERTZ, so the partials stop being whole-number multiples of anything and the pluck turns bell-like, metallic, clangorous. That inharmonicity is the feature. The +3 Hz variant is the other end of the same axis and sounds nothing like the others: too small to hear as a pitch change, it beats slowly against the dry signal - a slow shimmer that never repeats, because unlike a chorus there is no LFO to cycle. NOTE THE ASYMMETRY between up and down: shifting DOWN pushes low partials toward 0 Hz and folds any that would go negative back up (a 100 Hz partial shifted -200 Hz comes back at 100 Hz), which is why -200 sounds thicker and more chaotic than +200 rather than merely lower.",
